{
  "slug": "artist:project-pat",
  "name": "Project Pat",
  "editorial_article": "Born Patrick Earl Houston in 1973 in North Memphis, Tennessee, this pivotal figure in the Southern hip-hop landscape emerged as a core affiliate of Three 6 Mafia. His debut studio album Ghetty Green arrived in 1999 via his brother Juicy J's label Hypnotize Minds. He gained significant national recognition in 2000 by providing the hook for the Three 6 Mafia hit single Sippin' on Some Syrup. His 2001 release Mista Don't Play: Everythangs Workin featured the radio singles Chickenhead and Don't Save Her, which earned rotation on BET and MTV. Beyond his discography, Houston is an active philanthropist who leads the Collierville-based Go Foundation and conducts prison ministry sessions at facilities like the West TN State Penitentiary. In 2021, his contribution to Drake's Certified Lover Boy on the track Knife Talk reached number 4 on the Billboard Hot 100 chart. He has recorded across a wide array of labels including Loud, E1, and Taylor Gang, while maintaining his roots in the Memphis rap sound.",
